NAME
       passwd - the local user account file

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/passwd

DESCRIPTION
       /etc/passwd has one line per local account, readable by everyone. The
       seven fields are separated by colons:

           NAME:PASSWORD:UID:GID:COMMENT:HOME:SHELL

       NAME      the login name.
       PASSWORD  x, meaning the password (hash) is kept in /etc/shadow,
                 which only root can read.
       UID       the user ID number. 0 is root; 1-999 are system accounts;
                 ordinary users start at 1000 (see login.defs).
       GID       the number of the primary group (in /etc/group). New files
                 get this group.
       COMMENT   free text, usually the person's full name (GECOS field).
       HOME      the home directory.
       SHELL     the program started at login: /bin/bash normally;
                 /sbin/nologin refuses interactive logins.

       Change the file with useradd, usermod and userdel (or vipw), not with
       a plain editor. getent passwd NAME shows one account; id NAME shows
       its numbers and groups.

EXAMPLES
           student:x:1000:1000:Student User:/home/student:/bin/bash

           grep '^student:' /etc/passwd
           awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 {print $1}' /etc/passwd

SEE ALSO
       shadow(5), group(5), useradd(8), usermod(8), vipw(8), getent(1)
